ipeline: geometry repair (Houdini), automated UDIMs (RizomUV), Megascans database (Substance Designer), Virtual Texturing (UE5).',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:222:    tags: ['Pipeline', 'UDIM', 'Substance', 'UE5'],\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:227:    brand: 'Houdini · UE5',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:231:    tags: ['Houdini', 'Level Design', 'PCG', 'Heightfield'],\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:235:    title: 'Procedural Grass System',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:236:    brand: 'Houdini · Substance · UE5',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:240:    tags: ['Houdini', 'Substance', 'VAT', 'Vegetation'],\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:245:    brand: 'Unreal Engine 5',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:262:    title: 'Louisiana Bayou V1.5',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:263:    brand: 'Unreal Engine 5',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:272:    brand: 'Substance Designer',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:274:    descriptionEn: 'Procedural sedimentary rock material. Erosion, cracks, color variation. Custom sub-graphs, chunk breakup, fuzzy edges. 4K.',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:276:    tags: ['Substance', 'Procedural', 'Texturing'],\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:280:    title: 'Substance Designer MCP Plugin',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:282:    description: 'Plugin MCP pour Substance Designer. Description en langage naturel -> graphe PBR complet et fonctionnel. Open source.',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:283:    descriptionEn: 'MCP plugin for Substance Designer. Natural language → complete functional PBR graph. Open source.',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:285:    tags: ['MCP', 'Python', 'AI', 'Substance', 'Open Source'],\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:290:    brand: 'C++ · Unreal Engine 5',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:299:    brand: 'Unreal Engine 5',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:317:    brand: 'Houdini',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:319:    descriptionEn: 'Procedural water wheel geometry + FLIP water simulation. Parametric: size, bucket count, wood aging, moss growth.',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:321:    tags: ['Houdini', 'FLIP', 'Procedural', 'Simulation'],\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:325:    title: 'Pampa Grass HDA',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:326:    brand: 'Houdini HDA · UE5',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:330:    tags: ['Houdini', 'VEX', 'HDA', 'Vegetation', 'UE5'],\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:428:    ? 'Technical Artist specializing in Houdini + Unreal Engine 5. From procedural modeling to pipeline architecture - I design the systems studios need to scale production, automate workflows, and ship environments at a high visual standard. Tool development in Python, C++ and MCP. Bachelor Game Design LISAA Paris (2023) · Gnomon Online · CS50 Harvard (2024).'\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:429:    : \"Technical Artist specialise Houdini + Unreal Engine 5. De la modelisation procedurale a l'architecture pipeline - je concois les systemes dont les studios ont besoin pour scaler leur production et livrer a un haut niveau visuel. Developpement d'outils Python, C++ et MCP. Bachelor Game Design LISAA Paris (2023) · Gnomon Online · CS50 Harvard (2024).\";\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:485:                Technical Artist - Freelance\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:557:                3D / Procedural\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:646:                        color: e.type === 'Freelance' ? ACCENT3 : e.type === 'Diplome' ? '#10b981' : S[300],\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:60:  { type: \"text\", content: \"Lately I have been taking a week to build a custom C++ plugin for Unreal Engine 5.7 that lets AI agents manipulate the editor in real-time. Here is what I have learned.\" },\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:61:  { type: \"text\", content: \"These past weeks, I have been building a bridge between UE 5.7 and Cursor or Claude Code. The core idea is to let an agent generate and execute Python through Unreal\\u2019s Python Editor Scripting and spawn C++ classes and Blueprints directly inside the editor.\" },\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:63:  { type: \"text-image\", content: \"I have also been inspired by the recent work of Eldad Sery on Houdini and Cursor.\", image: `${P}/eldad-houdini.webp`, alt: \"Eldad Sery Houdini Cursor\", contain: true },\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:66:  { type: \"text\", content: \"Most existing Unreal MCP solutions work because they are locked to Claude Code, which excels at Python generation. Making the plugin compatible with lightweight models required heavy refactoring. Blueprint generation from agent output remains fragile. Each MCP handler needs multiple fallbacks because node graph serialization from raw text is still a minefield. It is buggy, it is experimental, and no GitHub release is planned.\" },\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:67:  { type: \"text\", content: \"Working daily with Houdini, I already live in a procedural, node-based mindset. And with models like Claude Opus 4.6 getting significantly better at sustained code reasoning, the trajectory is clear. These tools will only get more capable.\" },\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:94:        <p style={bodyStyle}>Custom C++ plugin for Unreal Engine 5.7 that lets AI agents manipulate the editor in real-time via MCP. The agent can query the scene, spawn and manipulate actors, and generate Blueprint classes. Scope: scene composition, blockout from image, procedural placement, and Blueprint scaffolding.</p>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:102:        <p style={{ ...bodyStyle, marginBottom: isMobile ? 32 : 48 }}>Drawing from open-source foundations and inspired by Gorka Games Studios and Eldad Sery's work on Houdini with Cursor. I did not want to copy and paste. I wanted to understand it. The plugin runs a server inside the editor, allowing an external agent to query the scene and generate actors.</p>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:109:            {imgZoom(\"eldad-houdini.webp\", \"Eldad Sery Houdini\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:110:            {captionBelow(\"Eldad Sery - Houdini and Cursor workflow\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:144:        {h2(\"Procedural Layout Generation\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:296:        <p style={bodyStyle}>I spent many hours making a glowing triangle in Unreal. So I built a tool with React that does the same thing in seconds. No SDF. No complex math. Just design, animate, export. React + SVG + Framer Motion - skip the Unreal material graph complexity.</p>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:312:          <div>{imgZoom(\"slide5.webp\", \"Export result\")}{captionBelow(\"PNG export - pixel-perfect match with the prototype for Unreal import\")}</div>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:466:          ⚠ Unrecoverable 2D/3D Assets · AI Coding Agents · Houdini · UE5\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:694:function MegascansToSubstanceSlides({ isMobile, lang }) {\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:834:            \"From Broken Mesh in Houdini to Megascans in Substance Painter\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:835:            \"Du Mesh Casse dans Houdini aux Megascans dans Substance Painter\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:840:            \"Creating high-quality hero assets for games requires a perfect balance between speed, output, and quality. For unique assets like a wooden bayou staircase, I built a complete pipeline: from salvaging broken geometry in Houdini to rendering Virtual Textures in Unreal Engine 5.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:841:            \"Creer des hero assets de haute qualite pour le jeu video requiert un equilibre parfait entre vitesse, rendu et qualite. Pour des assets uniques comme un escalier en bois de bayou, j'ai construit un pipeline complet : de la reparation de geometrie cassee dans Houdini au rendu en Virtual Textures dans Unreal Engine 5.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:844:        {pillRow([\"Houdini\", \"RizomUV\", \"Substance Designer\", \"Substance Painter\", \"Unreal Engine 5\"])}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:852:        {h2(t(\"Procedural Mesh Repair\", \"Reparation Procedurale du Mesh\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:855:            \"The workflow begins with raw, broken blockout meshes. Instead of manually fixing holes and non-manifold geometry, I rely on a procedural repair chain in Houdini. By using VDB from Polygons to create a watertight shell, PolyReduce to optimize density, and Clip & Mirror for perfect symmetry, I generate clean low-poly topology in seconds. This completely eliminates tedious manual retopology.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:856:            \"Le workflow commence avec des meshes de blockout bruts et casses. Au lieu de reparer manuellement les trous et la geometrie non-manifold, j'utilise une chaine de reparation procedurale dans Houdini. En utilisant VDB from Polygons pour creer une coque etanche, PolyReduce pour optimiser la densite, et Clip & Mirror pour une symetrie parfaite, je genere une topologie low-poly propre en quelques secondes.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:861:            {imgZoom(\"houdini-broken-stairs.webp\", \"Original broken stairs from Fab modular kit with Houdini node graph\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:862:            {captionBelow(t(\"I got this stairs from an old Unreal modular kit on Fab - I had to fix the model first, filling holes\", \"J'ai recupere cet escalier d'un ancien kit modulaire Unreal sur Fab - il fallait d'abord reparer le modele en comblant les trous\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:865:            {imgZoom(\"houdini-complete-graph.webp\", \"Complete Houdini repair graph\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:866:            {captionBelow(t(\"The complete repair graph in Houdini\", \"Le graph de reparation complet dans Houdini\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:875:            <SmartImg src={`${MS_BASE}/houdini-clip-vdb.webp`} alt=\"Clipped half-mesh in Houdini before VDB\" loading=\"lazy\" style={{ width: \"100%\", height: \"100%\", objectFit: \"cover\", objectPosition: \"center center\", display: \"block\", cursor: \"zoom-in\" }} onClick={() => setViewerSrc(`${MS_BASE}/houdini-clip-vdb.webp`)} />\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:878:            {t(\"Clip in Houdini for faster VDB. Mirror afterwards - think in components, not wasting compute.\", \"Clip dans Houdini pour un VDB plus rapide. Miroir apres - penser en composants, ne pas gaspiller le calcul.\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:915:            \"Before painting, I need a robust material library. Quixel Megascans (via Fab) offers incredible scanned surfaces, but Unreal Engine stores them as proprietary .uasset files with packed ORM textures. To bring these seamlessly into Substance Painter, I built a processing hub in Substance Designer:\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:916:            \"Avant de peindre, j'ai besoin d'une bibliotheque de materiaux solide. Quixel Megascans (via Fab) offre d'incroyables surfaces scannees, mais Unreal Engine les stocke en fichiers .uasset proprietaires avec des textures ORM packees. Pour les integrer dans Substance Painter, j'ai construit un hub de traitement dans Substance Designer :\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:922:            \"A custom Python script extracts BaseColor, Normal, Height, and ORM maps from Unreal, saving them as organized PNGs based on official Fab identifiers.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:923:            \"Un script Python personnalise extrait les maps BaseColor, Normal, Height et ORM depuis Unreal, les sauvegardant en PNG organises selon les identifiants officiels Fab.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:936:            \"The entire database is published as a single .sbsar archive. Every Megascans surface appears as a fully mapped, drag-and-drop material in Substance Painter.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:937:            \"Toute la base de donnees est publiee en une seule archive .sbsar. Chaque surface Megascans apparait comme un materiau entierement mappe, en drag-and-drop, dans Substance Painter.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:941:          {imgZoom(\"ue-megascans-materials.webp\", \"Unreal Engine Content Browser showing all Megascans material instances from Fab\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:942:          {captionBelow(t(\"Getting material instances from Fab and automating the texture export with Python before dropping them in Substance Designer\", \"Recuperation des instances de materiaux depuis Fab et automatisation de l'export des textures avec Python avant de les deposer dans Substance Designer\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:945:          {imgZoom(\"sd-graph-yewbark.webp\", \"Substance Designer graph for Yew Bark - individual graph with bitmap nodes, ORM channel shuffle, and PBR outputs with Integration Attributes\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:950:          {captionBelow(t(\"Import all materials from Substance Designer in a single .sbsar file - 2 GB uncompressed, reducible with compression\", \"Importer tous les materiaux depuis Substance Designer en un seul fichier .sbsar - 2 Go non compresse, reductible avec la compression\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:953:          {imgZoom(\"sp-sbsar-shelf.webp\", \"Substance Painter shelf with imported .sbsar Megascans materials\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:954:          {captionBelow(t(\"All materials available as drag-and-drop in Substance Painter shelf\", \"Tous les materiaux disponibles en drag-and-drop dans la shelf Substance Painter\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:966:            \"With the mesh imported and the Megascans library ready, texturing becomes purely artistic. I bake mesh maps at 4K resolution directly in Substance Painter, intentionally skipping the ZBrush high-poly phase. For standard architectural props, a clean low-poly bake combined with high-quality PBR materials is highly effective and saves hours of production time.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:967:            \"Avec le mesh importe et la bibliotheque Megascans prete, le texturing devient purement artistique. Je bake les mesh maps en 4K directement dans Substance Painter, en sautant intentionnellement la phase high-poly ZBrush. Pour les props architecturaux standard, un bake low-poly propre combine a des materiaux PBR haute qualite est tres efficace et economise des heures de production.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:972:            \"Using Black Masks and procedural generators (Dirt, Position, Curvature), I paint specific areas that need wear, moisture, and aging. Substance Painter is built for exactly this level of bespoke, granular artistic control.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:973:            \"En utilisant des Black Masks et des generateurs proceduraux (Dirt, Position, Curvature), je peins les zones specifiques qui necessitent usure, humidite et vieillissement. Substance Painter est concu exactement pour ce niveau de controle artistique granulaire et sur mesure.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:985:              {imgZoom(\"sp-dirt-layer-stack.webp\", \"Substance Painter layer stack with Dirt generator, Warp filter, and mask hierarchy\", \"right\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1003:            {captionBelow(t(\"Custom name automation for Substance Painter and Unreal Engine convention\", \"Automatisation du nommage pour la convention Substance Painter et Unreal Engine\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1020:            \"The textures are packed and exported as BaseColor (BC), ORM, and Normal (N) maps across 2048x2048 UDIM tiles. In Unreal Engine, Virtual Texturing must be enabled for UDIM support. The Master Material (M_Master_BC_ORM_N) uses Virtual Texture Parameters with correctly assigned Sampler Types: Virtual Color for BaseColor, Virtual Normal for the Normal map, and Linear Color for the ORM.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1021:            \"Les textures sont packees et exportees en maps BaseColor (BC), ORM et Normal (N) sur des tiles UDIM 2048x2048. Dans Unreal Engine, le Virtual Texturing doit etre active pour le support UDIM. Le Master Material (M_Master_BC_ORM_N) utilise des Virtual Texture Parameters avec les Sampler Types correctement assignes : Virtual Color pour le BaseColor, Virtual Normal pour la Normal map, et Linear Color pour l'ORM.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1026:          {captionBelow(t(\"In Unreal, beware to be on Virtual Color and Virtual Normal - wrong Sampler Types will break the material silently\", \"Dans Unreal, attention a etre sur Virtual Color et Virtual Normal - de mauvais Sampler Types casseront le materiau silencieusement\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1038:            \"Procedural Repair \\u00b7 Automated UDIMs \\u00b7 Virtual Textures\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1039:            \"Reparation Procedurale \\u00b7 UDIMs Automatises \\u00b7 Virtual Textures\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1044:            <SmartImg src={`${MS_BASE}/ue-result-front.webp`} alt=\"Final result in Unreal Engine\" loading=\"lazy\" style={{ width: \"100%\", height: \"100%\", objectFit: \"cover\", display: \"block\", cursor: \"zoom-in\" }} onClick={() => setViewerSrc(`${MS_BASE}/ue-result-front.webp`)} />\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1047:            <SmartImg src={`${MS_BASE}/ue-result-top.webp`} alt=\"Final result in Unreal Engine\" loading=\"lazy\" style={{ width: \"100%\", height: \"100%\", objectFit: \"cover\", display: \"block\", cursor: \"zoom-in\" }} onClick={() => setViewerSrc(`${MS_BASE}/ue-result-top.webp`)} />\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1050:        {captionBelow(t(\"Final result in Unreal Engine\", \"Resultat final dans Unreal Engine\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1057:        {eyebrow(t(\"Unreal Render\", \"Rendu Unreal\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1060:          {imgZoom(\"unreal-render-final.webp\", \"Final Unreal Engine render after Photoshop compositing\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1072:            {[\"Houdini\", \"RizomUV\", \"Substance Designer\", \"Substance Painter\", \"Unreal Engine 5\"].flatMap((tool, j) => [\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1099:   From Unreal PCG to Houdini HDA\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1211:              From Unreal PCG to Houdini HDA\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1216:              \"I built a new system handled with Houdini HDA because I was struggling with PCG in Unreal. My PC was frequently hitting low memory pressure, and it was taking a lot of time to set up and create multiple setups for every system of my environment - especially since Blueprints + PCG is still very laggy on my laptop.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1217:              \"J'ai construit un nouveau système géré avec un HDA Houdini parce que je galérais avec le PCG dans Unreal. Mon PC tombait fréquemment en low memory pressure, et ça prenait beaucoup de temps pour configurer chaque système de mon environnement - d'autant plus que Blueprints + PCG reste très lent sur mon laptop.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1228:        {/* ── HERO IMAGE - From Houdini to Unreal ── */}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1230:          {imgZoom(\"hero-houdini-to-unreal.webp\", \"From Houdini to Unreal - Full HDA Level Design\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1242:            }}>From Houdini to Unreal in One HDA</p>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1248:              \"Package all your Level Design and blockout into a single Houdini Digital Asset.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1249:              \"Regroupez tout votre Level Design et blockout dans un seul Houdini Digital Asset.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1263:            \"The complete scene blockout in Houdini's viewport. All assets are placed and scattered procedurally on the heightfield terrain - trees, vegetation, hero props, and the church house.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1264:            \"Le blockout complet de la scène dans le viewport Houdini. Tous les assets sont placés et scatterés procéduralement sur le terrain heightfield - arbres, végétation, props hero, et la church house.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1268:          {imgZoom(\"houdini-full-blockout.webp\", \"Houdini Full Blockout\", { mask: \"right\" })}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1277:        {h2(t(\"Import HDA in Unreal\", \"Import du HDA dans Unreal\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1280:            \"The Houdini Digital Asset is imported directly into Unreal Engine. Once cooked, every element from the HDA is available as individual actors in the scene - fully editable and ready for material assignment.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1281:            \"Le Houdini Digital Asset est importé directement dans Unreal Engine. Une fois cuit, chaque élément du HDA est disponible en tant qu'acteur individuel dans la scène - entièrement éditable et prêt pour l'assignation de matériaux.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1285:          {imgZoom(\"import-hda-unreal.webp\", \"Import HDA in Unreal Engine\", { mask: \"left\" })}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1420:            The better approach: bake an <strong style={{ color: \"#fff\" }}>opacity mask</strong> from the Heightfield Output (8-bit, red channel only), plug it into your Water Shader, and enable <strong style={{ color: \"#fff\" }}>Dither Temporal AA</strong> in Unreal.\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1423:            La meilleure approche : baker un <strong style={{ color: \"#fff\" }}>masque d'opacité</strong> depuis le Heightfield Output (8-bit, canal rouge uniquement), le brancher dans votre Water Shader, et activer <strong style={{ color: \"#fff\" }}>Dither Temporal AA</strong> dans Unreal.\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1445:          {imgZoom(\"softened-water-shader.webp\", \"Softened water borders in Unreal\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1480:            \"Heightfield Scatter distributes vegetation across the terrain using mask layers. Grass is scattered first, then palmettos reuse the same point cloud at a lower density, and trees get their own dedicated scatter pass.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1496:            { file: \"heightfield-scatter-grass.webp\", label: t(\"Heightfield Scatter\", \"Heightfield Scatter\"), sub: t(\"Grass and base vegetation\", \"Herbe et végétation de base\") },\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1550:            \"The 4 hero assets and all scattered vegetation are merged into a single output, then packaged as one Houdini Digital Asset. One file, one import, one cook - your entire level design in a single node.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1551:            \"Les 4 assets hero et toute la végétation scatterée sont fusionnés en une seule sortie, puis packagés en un seul Houdini Digital Asset. Un fichier, un import, un cook - tout votre level design dans un seul nœud.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1584:            { src: `${AS_BASE}/unreal-import-hda.webp`, label: t(\"Unreal Import HDA\", \"Import HDA dans Unreal\"), sub: t(\"Full scene cooked\", \"Scène complète cuite\") },\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1636:            <>If you use <strong style={{ color: \"#fff\" }}>Pack</strong> nodes before merging your assets, you can cook your HDA in Unreal and still keep your static meshes as individual assets. Incredibly convenient for texturing later on - each mesh gets its own material slot, just like importing FBX or OBJ files.</>,\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1637:            <>Si vous utilisez les nœuds <strong style={{ color: \"#fff\" }}>Pack</strong> avant de fusionner vos assets, vous pouvez cuire votre HDA dans Unreal tout en gardant vos static meshes en tant qu'assets individuels. Extrêmement pratique pour le texturing par la suite - chaque mesh obtient son propre slot de matériau, comme pour un import FBX ou OBJ.</>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1643:            \"Unreal Content Browser - individual static meshes baked from the HDA, ready for material assignment\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1644:            \"Unreal Content Browser - static meshes individuels bakés depuis le HDA, prêts pour l'assignation de matériaux\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1678:function LouisianaBayouChurchSlides({ isMobile, lang }) {\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1872:            alt=\"Louisiana Bayou Church\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:1934:              Louisiana<br />Bayou<br />Church\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2014:            alt=\"Louisiana Bayou Church Modeling\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2063:            Louisiana Bayou Church<br />\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2065:              {t(\"A Procedural Component System in Houdini\", \"Un Système de Composants Procédural dans Houdini\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2070:              \"A small wooden church, modeled in Houdini. Style from Red Dead Redemption II. Rural chapel, plain siding, shingled roof, a cross at the gable.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2071:              \"Une petite église en bois, modélisée dans Houdini. Style emprunté à Red Dead Redemption II. Chapelle rurale, bardage simple, toit en bardeaux, une croix au pignon.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2499:              Houdini · Church\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2504:                \"A procedural staircase HDA for the Louisiana Bayou Church.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2505:                \"Un escalier procedural en HDA pour la Louisiana Bayou Church.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2666:            <p style={{ fontFamily: \"'AnthropicMono', 'ui-monospace', monospace\", fontWeight: 500, fontSize: isMobile ? \"0.64rem\" : \"0.7rem\", letterSpacing: \"0.52em\", textTransform: \"uppercase\", color: \"rgba(255,255,255,0.45)\", margin: \"0 0 20px 0\" }}>Houdini</p>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2669:              {t(\"How to get varied planks without multi-UDIM. A procedural UV approach in Houdini.\", \"Comment avoir des planches variées sans multi-UDIM. Une approche UV procédurale dans Houdini.\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2700:            \"Disclaimer: this method is not the definitive solution, but it does allow in Houdini to repeat a mesh with a varied texture.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2701:            \"Disclaimer : cette méthode n'est pas l'alpha et l'oméga, elle permet néanmoins dans Houdini d'avoir une répétition d'un mesh avec une texture variée.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2714:            \"I installed RizomUV, but I quickly realized that for a repeatable tileable surface, RizomUV was not the right solution and was more suited to unique surfaces, especially since it creates a proxy, which is not ideal when working inside Houdini. I then tried to focus on UV Unwrap and UV Layout nodes placed before Copy to Points, because I told myself that if the UVs were computed beforehand, I could then distribute a more relevant 0-1 space across each copy.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2715:            \"J'ai installé RizomUV, mais je me suis rendu compte que pour une surface tileable répétable, RizomUV n'était pas la solution et était plus dédié aux surfaces uniques, sachant qu'il crée un proxy, ce qui n'est pas idéal en travaillant dans Houdini. J'ai ensuite essayé de me concentrer autour des nodes UV Unwrap et UV Layout placés avant le Copy to Points, car je me suis dit que si les UVs étaient calculés auparavant, je pourrais ensuite distribuer un espace 0-1 plus pertinent sur chaque copie.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2727:          {imgZoom(\"graph.webp\", \"Houdini graph\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2767:              {t(\"One mesh. Procedural naming. Infinite variations.\", \"Un mesh. Naming procédural. Variations infinies.\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2778:        {h2(t(\"Procedural UV Offset.\", \"Procedural UV Offset.\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2791:              [t(\"V1: Manual\", \"V1: Manuel\"), t(\"V2: Procedural\", \"V2: Procédural\")],\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2889:          {imgZoom(\"graph-comparison.webp\", \"Houdini graph old vs new\")}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2893:        <p style={{ fontFamily: \"'AnthropicMono', 'ui-monospace', monospace\", fontSize: \"0.65rem\", letterSpacing: \"0.2em\", textTransform: \"uppercase\", color: \"rgba(255,255,255,0.3)\", margin: \"14px 0 0\" }}>Houdini Graph</p>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:2911:        {h2(t(\"Procedural UV Offset.\", \"Procedural UV Offset.\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3090:function LouisianaBayouSlides({ isMobile, lang }) {\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3101:          {imgZoom(\"thumbnail.webp\", \"Louisiana Bayou V1.5\", { mask: \"left\" })}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3103:        <h1 style={titleStyle}>{t(\"Louisiana Bayou V1.5\", \"Louisiana Bayou V1.5\")}</h1>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3105:          \"A major technical update to the atmospheric environment project. Pushing deeper into UE5 rendering systems to bring a Louisiana swamp to life. The scene centers on an isolated wooden house half-submerged in bayou water, surrounded by towering swamp cypress trees draped in Spanish moss.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3127:          {captionBelow(t(\"Latest render of the bayou scene in Unreal Engine 5\", \"Dernier rendu de la scene du bayou dans Unreal Engine 5\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3192:          \"This project was born from an inspiration after watching the movie Sinners. I was immersed by the atmosphere and wanted to dive into the visual history of Louisiana.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3254:          \"Procedural cliff material created entirely in Substance Designer. Layered sedimentary rock with realistic erosion, cracks, and color variation. Built from scratch using custom subgraphs for tile layers, chunk breakup, and fuzzy edge detail at 4K resolution.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3255:          \"Materiau de falaise procedural entierement realise dans Substance Designer. Roche sedimentaire stratifiee avec erosion, fissures et variation de couleur. Construit de zero avec des sous-graphes custom en resolution 4K.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3366:          {imgZoom(\"thumbnail.webp\", \"Substance Designer MCP Plugin\", { mask: \"left\" })}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3368:        <h1 style={titleStyle}>{t(\"Substance Designer MCP Plugin\", \"Plugin MCP Substance Designer\")}</h1>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3370:          \"Describe a material in plain English and get a complete, working PBR graph inside Substance Designer. Built for technical artists who want to move faster without giving up control over the result. The agent handles the structure, you own the craft.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3371:          \"Decrivez un materiau en langage naturel, obtenez un graphe PBR complet et fonctionnel dans Substance Designer. Concu pour les artistes techniques. L'agent gere la structure, vous maitrisez le savoir-faire.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3417:          \"First Substance Designer fabric material study. Ornate woven pattern with procedural thread structure, weave variation, and surface relief. Built entirely from scratch: fiber direction, interlace logic, color variation per thread, and micro-detail normal pass.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3418:          \"Premiere etude de materiau tissu dans Substance Designer. Motif tisse orne avec structure de fil procedurale, variation de tissage et relief de surface. Direction des fibres, logique d'entrelacement, variation de couleur par fil, et passe normale micro-detail.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3482:function HoudiniGameEnvSlides({ isMobile, lang }) {\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3492:          {imgZoom(\"fractal-voronoi.webp\", \"Houdini Game Environments\", { mask: \"left\", radius: 0 })}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3494:        <h1 style={titleStyle}>{t(\"Houdini Game Environments\", \"Environnements Jeu Houdini\")}</h1>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3496:          \"Deep dive into creating assets and architecture for game environments, from Maya and ZBrush to Unreal Engine 5. Procedural modeling HDAs focusing on orientation, alignment, and rapid variation. Houdini handles alignment through the normal attribute - no manual rotation needed.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3497:          \"Plongee dans la creation d'assets et d'architecture pour les environnements de jeu. HDAs de modelisation procedurale axees sur l'orientation et l'alignement. Houdini gere l'alignement via l'attribut normal - pas de rotation manuelle.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3505:        {h2(t(\"Procedural Walls\", \"Murs proceduraux\"))}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3507:          {imgZoom(\"walls.webp\", \"Procedural Walls\", { mask: \"right\", radius: 0 })}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3530:          {imgZoom(\"/procedural-environments-hero.webp\", \"Procedural Environments\", { mask: \"right\", radius: 0 })}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3572:          \"Procedural planter modeled in Houdini. Quick variation of dimensions, material types, and ornamentation through parametric controls.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3573:          \"Bac de fleurs procedural modelise dans Houdini. Variation rapide des dimensions, types de materiaux et ornementations via des controles parametriques.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3750:        <h1 style={titleStyle}>{t(\"Procedural Water Wheel\", \"Roue a eau procedurale\")}</h1>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3752:          \"I deconstructed and divided several modules to build this fully procedural waterwheel geometry in Houdini, before integrating a water simulation (FLIP). The objective: create a flexible system designed to evolve with production feedback. Turning technical complexity into an iterative creative tool. A huge thank you to David Silberbauer and Gnomon for this foundational workshop. Shared knowledge is an invaluable asset.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3753:          \"J'ai deconstruit et divise plusieurs modules pour construire cette geometrie de roue a eau entierement procedurale dans Houdini, avant d'integrer une simulation d'eau (FLIP). L'objectif : creer un systeme flexible concu pour evoluer avec les retours de production. Un grand merci a David Silberbauer et Gnomon pour cet atelier fondateur.\"\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3809:          {imgZoom(\"slide1.webp\", \"Procedural Rock Gen\", { mask: \"right\" })}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3811:        <h1 style={titleStyle}>{t(\"Procedural Rock Gen\", \"Generation de rochers procedurale\")}</h1>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3840:function PampaGrassSlides2({ isMobile, lang }) {\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3850:          {imgZoom(\"thumbnail.webp\", \"Pampa Grass\", { mask: \"left\" })}\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3852:        <h1 style={titleStyle}>{t(\"Pampa Grass HDA\", \"Pampa Grass HDA\")}</h1>\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3865:          \"The HDA is divided into four modules: Fiber, Spine, Stems, and Grass. Every module shares attributes with the others. The Grass Scattering System shares the same logic as the Stems. VEX is the heart of this tool - it handles natural curls, noise-driven orientation, and attribute randomization.\",\nsrc/projectSlides.jsx:3871:            {captionBelow(t(\"Complete Pampa Grass HDA - infinite variations from one node\", \"HDA Pampa Grass complet - variations infinies a partir d'un noeud\"))}\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"public/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:33:     \"text\": \"実写収録でモーションキャプチャをやったことがあるなら、最初のパスできれいなアニメーションを動画から取り出すのがどれほど面倒か知っているはずだ。動画の中にある動きを、そのままゲームエンジンの中へ持ち込む。それを解決してくれたのが Unreal と Epic Games の魔法使いたちのチームである。ここで見せるのは、YouTube のゲームプレイ動画から取った 4 秒の攻撃アニメーションだ。1080p 30 fps から、より良い結果を求めるなら待ち時間と引き換えに 2160p 60 fps まで取り込める。処理はどのマシンでもローカルで走る（この記事の主題は、公式には対応していない macOS でそれを走らせる方法である。プラグインはまだ実験段階だが、16 本ほどの独自アニメーションのために 24 時間動かし続けても、バグには一度も出会わなかった）。そしてその同じ動きを、Unreal の自分のキャラクターに載せる。スーツもスタジオも俳優もいらない。動画だけである。以下がその結果で、まず素の Unreal マネキン、次に解析中の MetaHuman に載せたものである。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:225:     \"text\": \"このプラグインは Epic Games のものである。Unreal Engine とともに配布されており、そのコードに私が書いた部分は一行もない。私の貢献は狭く、はっきり書いておきたい。macOS でコンパイルし、実際のクリップに対して端から端まで走らせ、アニメーションが出てくるところまで確認した。動く。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:248:     \"text\": \" の Unreal Engine 5.8.1、Xcode 26.6。入力は 3840x2160、60 フレーム毎秒のゲームプレイ映像 4 秒。出力は 5 つの処理ステージを経た 239 フレームのトラッキング結果で、所要時間はおよそ 30 分だった。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:402:     \"text\": \"。ライブラリに追加し、Epic Games Launcher から Unreal Engine 5.8 のインストール先に導入する。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:558:     \"text\": \"と告げ、エンジンモジュールは実行時にコンパイルできないと続ける。行き止まりに見えるが、そうではない。モジュール自体は存在しており、5.8 の以前のビルドに対してコンパイルされているだけである。Unreal はビルド識別子が一致しないものをすべて拒否する。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:654:     \"text\": \"Unreal 内蔵の動画リーダーは普通の H.264 ファイルを受け付けない。ほとんどのカメラも画面録画ソフトも、まさにそれを吐き出すというのに。設定を一行足せば、デコードはすでに手元にあるツールへ委ねられる。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:1277:     \"text\": \"と言うとき、どのボーンが左ひじなのかを知っている必要がある。そしてその答えはキャラクターごとに違う。MetaHuman と Unreal のマネキンと腕が 4 本ある怪物とで、ボーンの一覧は共通ではない。だから、あるスケルトン向けに作られたアニメーションは、誰かが訳してやるまで、別のスケルトンにとって何の意味も持たない。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:1345:     \"text\": \" が行う。どのボーンがどれに対応するかを言うためだけにある Unreal のアセットである。Epic は両方を出来合いの形でプラグインに同梱しているので、自分で作る必要はなく、使うだけでよい。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:1469:     \"text\": \"よい知らせは、テイクがいったん Unreal のスケルトンに乗れば、どこへでも運べることである。上の灰色マネキンははじめから予定していたものではない。リターゲッター一つでそこまで届いた。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:1749:     \"text\": \"上のクリップは、書き出したアニメーションを標準のマネキンで再生したものである。SMPL を経由する回り道の意味はそこにある。テイクがいったん Unreal のスケルトンに乗れば、IK Retargeter が運べる先ならどこへでも行く。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.ja.json:1819:     \"text\": \"どのシェルからでもエディタを動かせる小さな MCP クライアントと、Unreal のクラッシュを検知し、クラッシュレポーターを閉じ、ポートを確認して起動し直すガードが入っている。長いアセット移行や 30 分かかるソルブは、いずれエンジンのアサートに出くわす。ガードがなければ、作業はそこで止まったままになる。\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:33:     \"text\": \" Si vous faites déjà de la mocap en séance live, vous savez que sortir les premières animations de vos vidéos peut être pénible avant d'avoir quelque chose de propre sur les premières passes. Prendre un mouvement qui existe sur une vidéo et l'avoir directement dans le moteur de jeu, c'est ce que l'équipe de magiciens d'Unreal et d'Epic Games résout ! Dans cette démonstration, une animation d'attaque de quatre secondes tirée d'une vidéo de gameplay sur YouTube, à récupérer en 1080p 30 fps et jusqu'en 2160p et 60 fps pour de meilleurs résultats au prix d'une attente plus longue, à faire tourner en local sur n'importe quelle machine (tout l'objet de cet article est de savoir comment la faire tourner sur macOS, qui n'est pas pris en charge nativement, même si le plugin reste expérimental et que je n'ai rencontré aucun bug en le faisant tourner 24 heures sur 24 pour une quinzaine d'autres animations personnalisées), et poser ce même mouvement sur mon propre personnage dans Unreal. Sans combinaison, sans studio, sans acteur. Rien que la vidéo. Voici le résultat, d'abord sur un mannequin Unreal ordinaire, puis sur un MetaHuman pendant le calcul.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:225:     \"text\": \"Le plugin appartient à Epic Games. Il est livré avec Unreal Engine et aucune ligne de son code n'est de moi. Ma contribution est étroite, et je préfère l'énoncer platement : je l'ai compilé sur macOS et je l'ai fait tourner de bout en bout sur un vrai clip jusqu'à ce qu'une animation en sorte. Cela fonctionne.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:236:     \"text\": \"Configuration : macOS sur Apple Silicon, Unreal Engine 5.8.1 en \",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:402:     \"text\": \". Ajoutez-le à votre bibliothèque, puis installez-le depuis l'Epic Games Launcher dans votre installation d'Unreal Engine 5.8.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:558:     \"text\": \" et qu'un module de moteur ne peut pas être compilé à l'exécution. Cela ressemble à une impasse, mais n'en est pas une : les modules sont bien là, ils ont seulement été compilés pour une version antérieure de 5.8, et Unreal refuse tout ce dont l'identifiant de build ne correspond pas.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:654:     \"text\": \" Les lecteurs vidéo d'Unreal refusent les fichiers H.264 ordinaires, c'est-à-dire ce que produisent presque toutes les caméras et tous les enregistreurs d'écran. Une ligne de configuration confie le décodage à un outil que vous possédez déjà.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:1277:     \"text\": \", elle a besoin de savoir quel os est le coude gauche, et la réponse change d'un personnage à l'autre. Un MetaHuman, le mannequin d'Unreal et un monstre à quatre bras ne partagent pas la même liste d'os. Une animation faite pour un squelette ne veut donc rien dire pour un autre tant que personne ne la traduit.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:1345:     \"text\": \", un asset Unreal dont l'unique rôle est de dire quel os correspond à quel autre. Epic livre les deux tout prêts à l'intérieur du plugin, si bien que vous n'avez pas à les construire mais seulement à vous en servir :\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:1469:     \"text\": \"La bonne nouvelle, c'est qu'une fois la prise arrivée sur un squelette Unreal, elle voyage. Le mannequin gris ci-dessus n'était pas prévu au programme ; un seul retargeter a suffi pour l'atteindre.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:1749:     \"text\": \" Le clip ci-dessus est l'animation exportée, rejouée sur un mannequin standard, ce qui fait tout l'intérêt du détour par SMPL : une fois la prise arrivée sur un squelette Unreal, elle va partout où un IK Retargeter peut l'emmener.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.fr.json:1819:     \"text\": \"Il contient aussi un petit client MCP pour piloter l'éditeur depuis n'importe quel shell, et un garde-fou qui détecte un crash d'Unreal, ferme le rapporteur d'erreur, vérifie le port et relance. Une longue migration d'assets ou une résolution de trente minutes finit toujours par rencontrer une assertion du moteur, et sans garde-fou la tâche s'arrête simplement là.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:33:     \"text\": \" If you are already making mocap from live sessions, you know that taking the first animations out of your videos can be a pain to have something clean on the first passes. Take a movement that exists on a video and having it directly inside the game engine is what Unreal Epic Games' team of magicians solves! In this showcase, a four second attack animation from a gameplay video from YouTube, get it on 1080p 30 fps up to 2160p and 60 fps for better results at the cost of longer waiting time, run it locally on any machine (the point of this article is to know how to run it on macOS, which is not supported natively, though the plugin is still experimental and I did not experience any bug running it 24/24 for around 16 more custom animations), and get that same movement onto my own character in Unreal. No suit, no studio, no actor. Just the video. Below is the result, first on a plain Unreal mannequin, then on a MetaHuman during the solve.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:225:     \"text\": \"The plugin belongs to Epic Games. It ships with Unreal Engine and none of its code is mine. My contribution is narrow and I want it stated plainly: I compiled it on macOS and ran it end to end on a real clip until an animation came out. It works.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:236:     \"text\": \"Setup: macOS on Apple Silicon, Unreal Engine 5.8.1 as an \",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:402:     \"text\": \". Add it to your library, then install it from the Epic Games Launcher into your Unreal Engine 5.8 installation.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:558:     \"text\": \", and that engine modules cannot be compiled at runtime. It reads like a dead end. It is not: the modules are there, they were simply compiled against an earlier build of 5.8, and Unreal refuses anything whose build identifier does not match.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:654:     \"text\": \" Unreal's own video readers refuse ordinary H.264 files, which is what almost every camera and every screen recorder produces. One line of configuration hands the decoding to a tool you already have.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:1032:     \"text\": \" built with a different engine version, and only the second was plausible given those dylibs. Unreal stamps every compiled module with a build identifier and refuses to load anything that does not match. The plugin announced \",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:1546:     \"text\": \", it needs to know which bone is the left elbow, and the answer differs from one character to the next. A MetaHuman, Unreal's mannequin and a monster with four arms do not share a bone list. So an animation made for one skeleton means nothing to another until somebody translates it.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:1614:     \"text\": \", an Unreal asset whose only job is to say which bone corresponds to which. Epic ships both of them ready made inside the plugin, so you do not build them, you just use them:\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:1738:     \"text\": \"The good news is that once the take reaches an Unreal skeleton, it travels. The grey mannequin above was never part of the plan; it took one retargeter to get there.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:2018:     \"text\": \" The clip above is the exported animation replaying on a stock mannequin, which is the whole point of the SMPL detour: once the take reaches an Unreal skeleton, it goes wherever an IK Retargeter can take it.\",\npublic/articles/metahuman-markerless-macos.json:2088:     \"text\": \"It includes a small MCP client for driving the editor from any shell, and a guard that detects an Unreal crash, closes the reporter, checks the port and relaunches. A long asset migration or a thirty minute solve will eventually meet an engine assert, and without a guard the task simply stops there.\",\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:3:   Matthieu Huguet · Technical Artist\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:4:   1:1 match of FreelanceDashboard CVView.tsx\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:31:  { name: 'Houdini',            level: 5, category: 'core',   tags: ['VEX', 'HDAs', 'Procedural', 'UV', 'FLIP', 'Heightfields'] },\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:32:  { name: 'Unreal Engine 5',    level: 5, category: 'engine', tags: ['Blueprint', 'PCG', 'Material/Shader', 'UMG', 'Niagara'] },\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:35:  { name: 'Substance Designer', level: 4, category: 'core',   tags: ['Procedural PBR', 'Node Graph', 'MCP Plugin'] },\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:37:  { name: 'Substance Painter',  level: 4, category: 'core',   tags: ['Texturing', 'Baking', 'PBR'] },\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:53:    title: 'Technical Artist & Designer',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:56:    type: 'Freelance',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:60:      'Workflow Houdini-to-UE5 pour environment set dressing rapide et scalable.',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:68:      'Houdini-to-UE5 workflow for fast, scalable environment set dressing.',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:76:    title: 'Technical Artist',\nsrc/components/CVPage.jsx:79:    type: 'Freelance',\nsrc/components/CVPag